---
import Cover from '../layouts/Cover.astro';
---

<Cover
  title="Method"
  description="How Noemium grades tools: ship / situational / skip, what counts as a receipt, and why a PR is the unit of truth."
  page="method"
>
  <div class="cover-doc">
    <section class="cover-lede">
      <h1>The ledger, <em>not the vibe</em>.</h1>
      <p>
        How a card earns a verdict, a receipt, and a place on the floor. The unit
        of truth is a pull request — one file, one observer, one date.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="cover-doors">
      <div class="cover-door">
        <b>Ship</b>
        <span>We run it and would run it again for the job on the card. Limitations are still printed — ship is not a trophy.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cover-door">
        <b>Situational</b>
        <span>It wins in a named setup and loses outside it. Read the limitations before you pay.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cover-door">
        <b>Skip</b>
        <span>We would pick something else for the same job. The card stays so you can see why — and who we point to instead, when we have a peer.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <article class="cover-prose">
      <h2>Receipts</h2>
      <p>
        A receipt is a URL that backs a printed fact. Pricing pages first. Vendor docs, licenses,
        and git repos count. A marketing homepage is a weak receipt: we still show it, we just
        don't let it pretend to be a price.
      </p>
      <p>
        If we can't show a source, we don't print the number. Click any VERIFIED stamp for the
        date, the observer, the footnotes, and the file on GitHub.
      </p>

      <h2>Verification</h2>
      <p>
        Source-verified means receipts back every printed fact. Field-tested means we ran it
        hands-on. The <a href="/verified/">verified shelf</a> lists only field-tested cards;
        empty counts are shown honestly.
      </p>

      <h2>Briefing</h2>
      <p>
        A flagship card can carry a briefing: named strengths, when to use it, when to skip it,
        next to the limitations. All three or none. It is still one YAML file and one PR — not a
        score, not a vote, not a live ticker. Price history is the git log of this repo.
      </p>

      <h2>No stars</h2>
      <p>
        A 4.3 from nine reviews is an invoice costume. We don't average vibes. Wrong? Edit the
        page. The history is public.
      </p>

      <h2>Agents</h2>
      <p>
        A card lands in <a href="/agents/">/agents</a> only when the tool shows technical agency,
        not when the vendor calls it one. We look for a tool-use loop, persistent memory across
        turns, scheduling, or unattended runs. A chatbot that answers questions is a tool. The
        same model wrapped in a loop that plans, calls tools, and retries until a job is done is
        an agent.
      </p>

      <h2>Paid placements</h2>
      <p>
        Zero. Anchor cards on the floor are an editorial mix so the shelf isn't only famous
        names — not a slot you can buy. Affiliate links are declared in the YAML and marked on
        the page. They never move a verdict. Every declined paid-influence offer is logged in the{' '}
        <a href="/refusals/">Open Refusal Ledger</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/about/">About</a>
        {' · '}
        <a href="/why/">Why directories lie</a>
        {' · '}
        <a href="/status/">Status</a>
        {' · '}
        <a href="/contribute/">Contribute a card</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Cover>
